#%PAM-1.0

# Lockscreen password-only auth service — see auditorias/autenticacion/README.md
# §11. LockAuth.qml's password PamContext used to reuse the system "passwd"
# service, which `include`s system-auth — and system-auth's own auth stack
# then started with `auth sufficient pam_fprintd.so timeout=4`. That meant every
# lockscreen password attempt silently ran an up-to-4-second fingerprint
# check FIRST, blocking the real password check behind it — and touching the
# sensor during that window unlocked instantly with NO password check at
# all, completely bypassing the launcher's ">security" fingerprint-enabled
# toggle (which only gates Quickshell's own separate `quickshell-fprint`
# PamContext, not this system-wide stack).
#
# Byte-for-byte identical to system-auth's own auth section — and system-auth
# itself carries no fingerprint, so neither this service nor su/a TTY login
# ever wait on the reader. sudo adds it in its own service (system/pam.d/sudo).
# No password/session sections: PamContext only calls pam_authenticate here
# (re-auth to unlock an already-open session, not a fresh login), same minimal
# shape already used by quickshell-fprint's own PAM service.
auth        required                    pam_faillock.so         preauth
-auth       [success=2 default=ignore]  pam_systemd_home.so
auth        [success=1 default=bad]     pam_unix.so             try_first_pass nullok
auth        [default=die]               pam_faillock.so         authfail
auth        optional                    pam_permit.so
auth        required                    pam_env.so
auth        required                    pam_faillock.so         authsucc

account     include     system-login
